\label{m0015_Lorentz_Force} % <-- Must match name of this file and module directory name.  Don't mess with this!
\index{Lorentz force|(}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%ORB HIGHLIGHT
\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=yellow!20,nobreak=true] 
The \emph{Lorentz force} is the force experienced by charge in the presence of electric and magnetic fields.  
\end{mdframed}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Consider a particle having charge $q$.
The force ${\bf F}_e$ experienced by the particle in the presence of electric field intensity 
\index{electric field intensity}
${\bf E}$ is 
$$ {\bf F}_e = q{\bf E} $$
The force ${\bf F}_m$ experienced by the particle in the presence of magnetic flux density 
\index{magnetic flux density}
${\bf B}$ is
$$ {\bf F}_m = q{\bf v} \times {\bf B} $$  
where ${\bf v}$ is the velocity of the particle.
The Lorentz force experienced by the particle is simply the sum of these forces; i.e.,
%
\begin{flalign}   
{\bf F} &= {\bf F}_e + {\bf F}_m \nonumber \\
        &= q\left( {\bf E} + {\bf v} \times {\bf B} \right) \label{m0015_eLF}
\end{flalign}
%
The term ``Lorentz force'' is simply a concise way to refer to the \emph{combined} contributions of the electric and magnetic fields.

A common application of the Lorentz force concept is in analysis of the motions of charged particles in electromagnetic fields.
The Lorentz force causes charged particles to exhibit distinct rotational (``cyclotron'') 
\index{cyclotron motion}
and translational (``drift'') motions.
This is illustrated in Figures~\ref{m0015_Charged-particle-drifts} and \ref{m0015_Cyclotron_motion}.
%
